Think of GNSS like a planet-sized network of super-accurate clocks on satellites; your phone can read that clock to time-stamp a location fix down to nanoseconds. You can do it the easy way (a normal UTC timestamp on each location fix) or the nerdy way (the raw GNSS clock in nanoseconds). Below are the steps for both, so you can choose how deep you want to go.

Easy way (good enough for most apps):
- Require ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION, ask the user for permission at runtime.
- Get a location fix (e.g., with FusedLocationProviderClient or LocationManager).
- Read location.getTime() which is the UTC timestamp in milliseconds for when the fix was made.
- Done. This is clean, battery-friendly, and plenty precise for common needs.

Raw GNSS clock (nanosecond-level, Android 7.0+):
- Make sure the device supports GPS: PackageManager.FEATURE_LOCATION_GPS.
- Add and request ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION permission.
- Get LocationManager and register a GnssMeasurementsEvent.Callback.
- In onGnssMeasurementsReceived, read the GNSS clock:
  GnssClock clock = event.getClock();
  long gnssTimeNanos = clock.getTimeNanos(); // raw GNSS receiver time (nanoseconds)
- If you need human-readable UTC time from the raw clock, combine timeNanos with fullBiasNanos/biasNanos and (optionally) leap seconds from the clock to convert to GPS time, then to UTC. If that sounds intense, prefer the easy way with location.getTime().
- Unregister the callback when you’re done (onPause/onStop) to save battery.

Minimal example (raw GNSS):
LocationManager lm = (LocationManager) getSystemService(Context.LOCATION_SERVICE);
GnssMeasurementsEvent.Callback cb = new GnssMeasurementsEvent.Callback() {
  @Override
  public void onGnssMeasurementsReceived(GnssMeasurementsEvent event) {
    GnssClock clock = event.getClock();
    long gnssTimeNanos = clock.getTimeNanos();
    // Optional: use clock.getFullBiasNanos(), clock.getBiasNanos(), and clock.getLeapSecond()
    // to convert to UTC if you truly need it.
  }
};
lm.registerGnssMeasurementsCallback(cb);

Tip: If you just want a reliable timestamp alongside latitude/longitude, use location.getTime(); if you’re doing research or high-precision navigation, use the GNSS nanosecond clock and do the conversion.

